045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186149432 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186149432 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6b93d037-203c-56ae-93ec-c5c47c02817e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186149433 #edb/ref 1080 "is not equivalent to" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186149433 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186149433 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "61068af2-78bc-5035-b5c4-5c4d84354b76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186149434 #edb/ref 1080 "is equivalent to" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186149434 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186149434 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "76eb381c-b19c-5cb2-9da5-aca686b85701" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151842 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14588" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151842 #edb/ref 1071 "If $ka \\equiv kb \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\,7)$\nis equivalent to $%\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad a \\equiv b \\:(\\textrm{mod} \\,7)$,\nwhich of the following could be the value of $k$?\n\n1. $k=-12$\n2. $k=4$\n3. $k=-14$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151842 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c638e469-4c15-52f3-a397-a7aac86d654e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151842 #edb/ref 1074 "The division property of modular arithmetic states that\n\n$$\nka\\equiv kb\\quad(\\textrm{mod} \\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\, n)\n$$\n\nprovided that $k$ and $n$ are coprime. In other words, we require\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, the modulus $n=7$. Let's consider each value of $k$ in turn.\n\n- If $k=-12$, then $\\text{gcd}(k,n) = \\text{gcd}(-12,7) = 1$. Therefore, our equivalence is valid for $k=-12$.\n- If $k=4$, then $\\text{gcd}(k,n) = \\text{gcd}(4,7) = 1$. Therefore, our equivalence is valid for $k=4$.\n- If $k=-14$, then $\\text{gcd}(k,n) = \\text{gcd}(-14,7) = 7\\neq 1$. Therefore, our equivalence is *not* valid for $k=-14$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151843 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14589" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151843 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $4a \\equiv 4b \\,(\\textrm{mod}\\,8)$, which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n1. $a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,4)$\n2. $a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,2)$\n3. $a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,8)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151843 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9f2134e8-62dd-5748-a202-819b4ca5208f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151843 #edb/ref 1074 "The division property of modular arithmetic states that for\n\n$$\nk\\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\nka\\equiv kb\\quad(\\textrm{mod} \\,kn) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\, n)\n$$\n\nWe're asked to consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n4a \\equiv 4b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,8)\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(4,8) =4\n$$\n\nApplying the division property, we have the following:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4a & ≡4b & (mod8) \\\\ 4 \\cdot a & ≡4 \\cdot b & (mod4 \\cdot 2) \\\\ 4 \\cdot a & ≡4 \\cdot b & (mod4 \\cdot 2) \\\\ a & ≡b & (mod2)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151858 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14612" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151858 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $15a \\equiv 15b \\,(\\textrm{mod}\\,20)$, which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n1. $a \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\, 20)$\n2. $a \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,4)$\n3. $7a \\equiv 7b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,4)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151858 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "344211e7-11c0-54f9-8a78-e64ff53deff0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151858 #edb/ref 1074 "The division properties of modular arithmetic state the following:\n\n- For $k\\neq 0$, we have\n$ka \\equiv kb \\quad(\\text{mod}\\,kn) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$.\n- For $\\text{gcd}(k, n) = 1$, we have\n$ka \\equiv kb \\quad(\\text{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Applying the first property, we have the following:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}15a & ≡15b & (mod20) \\\\ 5 \\cdot 3 \\cdot a & ≡5 \\cdot 3 \\cdot b & (mod5 \\cdot 4) \\\\ 5 \\cdot 3 \\cdot a & ≡5 \\cdot 3 \\cdot b & (mod5 \\cdot 4) \\\\ 3a & ≡3b & (mod4)\\end{bmatrix}$\nSince $\\gcd(3,4)=1$, by the second property, we have:\n$3a \\equiv 3b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,4) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,4)$\n- Statement III is true. By statement II and the second property, we have:\n$a≡b(mod4)⟺7a≡7b(mod4)$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152094 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152094 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152479 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152094 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152094 #edb/ref 1039 "Using the First Division Property" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152094 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152094 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "bca70f6c-d087-5b8e-aff2-581adca9ed1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152095 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152095 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152095 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152095 #edb/ref 1039 "Using the Second Division Property" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152095 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152095 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4f567e86-63a5-512d-b830-be97bedcf0c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152096 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152096 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152096 #edb/ref 1039 "Combining the Division Properties" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152096 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152096 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "3822141e-3cea-5c34-b925-a522c2273335" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152342 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186152957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152342 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186152958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152342 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186152959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152342 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186152960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152342 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186152961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152342 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186152962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152342 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152342 #edb/ref 1045 "The Division Properties of Modular Arithmetic" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152342 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186152957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152342 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152342 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "4d0ed4da-8ca5-550b-8192-8108992e2e42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152477 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149414 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152477 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152477 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152477 #edb/ref 1069 "q-212053" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152477 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following congruence:\n\n$5 \\cdot 2a≡5 \\cdot 2b(mod18)$\n\nSince $5$ and {{field-1}} are {{field-2}}, this congruence {{field-3}} $2a≡2b(mod18)$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152477 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "826a8410-28c1-5981-bb78-74772400c329" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152478 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152478 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152478 #edb/ref 1069 "q-212054" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152478 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following congruence:\n\n$2 \\cdot 3a≡2 \\cdot 3b(mod16)$\n\nSince $2$ and $16$ are {{field-1}}, this congruence {{field-2}} $3a≡3b(mod16)$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152478 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4dbb180b-acf0-5684-81e4-b820ca5ce13c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152957 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152957 #edb/ref 1061 154648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152957 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "105aa43c-c166-5fbc-95f3-769e0f6da6c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152957 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152958 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152958 #edb/ref 1061 154649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152958 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ac11f463-25aa-55b9-9ac0-a331f041dc4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152958 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152959 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152959 #edb/ref 1061 154652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152959 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1b9d1463-a78c-5122-b686-fb1e6073e599" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152959 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152960 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152960 #edb/ref 1061 154653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152960 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "da4afd2b-3aae-5054-a973-b9b456caa174" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152960 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152095 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152961 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152961 #edb/ref 1061 154657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152961 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f823d7c8-2ace-5f65-b27c-2b9e00e71538" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152961 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152096 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152962 #edb/ref 1061 154660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152962 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "10408cde-12fb-5b1c-9dcd-ef994ab66bbb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152962 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153452 #edb/ref 1064 "In a previous lesson, we saw that congruences obey another scalar multiplication property:\n\n$$\na\\equiv b \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nThe (second) **division property** of modular arithmetic tells us that\n\n$$\nka\\equiv kb\\quad(\\textrm{mod} \\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\, n)\n$$\n\n*provided that $k$ and $n$ are coprime*. In other words, we require\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(k,n)=1\n$$\n\n**Caution:** The condition that $k$ and $n$ are coprime is *very* important! The division property does *not* work when $k$ and $n$ are not coprime!\n\nThis might seem a little puzzling, so let's look at some concrete examples:\n\n- Consider the following true statement:\n$12 \\equiv 2 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:5)$\nNotice that the two numbers in our congruence have a common factor of ${\\color{blue}2}{:}$\n${\\color{blue}2} \\cdot 6 \\equiv {\\color{blue}2} \\cdot 1 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:5)$\nSince $\\textrm{gcd}({\\color{blue}2},5)=1$, we can cancel out ${\\color{blue}2}$ from both sides, keeping the modulus unchanged.\n$6 \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:5)$\n- Next, consider the following true statement:\n$12 \\equiv 6 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:6)$\nNotice that the two numbers in our congruence, *and the modulus*, have a common factor of ${\\color{red}3}{:}$\n${\\color{red}3} \\cdot 4 \\equiv {\\color{red}3} \\cdot 2 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:6)$\nBecause $\\textrm{gcd}({\\color{red}3},6) = 3 \\neq 1$, we *cannot* cancel out ${\\color{red}3}$ from both sides. If we do, we get the following *false* statement!\n$4\\equiv 2 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:6)$.\nIn such cases, we should use the first division property to simplify the congruence." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153452 #edb/ref 1065 9018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153452 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b53a59cc-bae8-5427-90c1-8c6ec21119a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153452 #edb/ref 1067 "The Second Division Property" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153453 #edb/ref 1064 "In a previous lesson, we saw that congruences obey the following scalar multiplication property:\n\n$$\na\\equiv b \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,kn)\n$$\n\nIt turns out that for\n\n$$\nk\\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe can perform this operation in reverse. That is,\n\n$$\nka \\equiv kb \\quad(\\text{mod}\\,kn) \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nTherefore, for\n\n$$\nk\\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe have the following **division property,** which we can write as an equivalence:\n\n$$\nka \\equiv kb \\quad(\\text{mod}\\,kn) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the following true statement:\n\n$$\n12 \\equiv 6 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:6)\n$$\n\nNote that all three numbers in our congruence have a common factor of ${\\color{blue}3}$.\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3} \\cdot 4 \\equiv {\\color{blue}3} \\cdot 2 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:{\\color{blue}3} \\cdot 2)\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the division property, we can cancel a factor of ${\\color{blue}3}$ from both sides and the modulus.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\cdot 4≡3 \\cdot 2 & (mod3 \\cdot 2) \\\\ 4≡2 & (mod2)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIt's important to note that the division property does not hold if $k=0$. For example, consider the following counterexample.\n\n$$\n0\\cdot 2 \\equiv 0\\cdot 1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad 2\\equiv 1 \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,2)\n$$\n\nThe antecedent is true, the consequent is false, and hence the implication is *false*." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153453 #edb/ref 1065 9019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153453 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "80aec645-9a36-5a86-a866-dedff95e8091" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153453 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153454 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's see why the division properties work.\n\n- The proof of the first property is straightforward:\n$ka≡kb(modkn) \\mid ⟺kn\\begin{vmatrix}(ka - kb) \\\\ ⟺kn\\end{vmatrix}k(a - b); ⟺kn\\begin{vmatrix}k(a - b) \\\\ ⟺n\\end{vmatrix}(a - b); ⟺a≡b(modn)$\n\n- To prove the second division property, suppose that $\\textrm{gcd}(k,n)=1$ and ${\\color{blue}k}a\\equiv {\\color{blue}k}b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\,n)$. Then, according to the definition of congruent integers, we obtain\n$ka≡kb(modn) \\mid ⟺n\\begin{vmatrix}(ka - kb) \\\\ ⟺n\\end{vmatrix}k(a - b)$.\nSince $k$ and $n$ are coprime and $k(a - b)$ is divisible by $n$, we have that $(a - b)$ must be divisible by $n$. Now, using the definition again, we get\n$n \\mid (a - b) \\mid ⟺a≡b(modn)$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153454 #edb/ref 1065 9021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153454 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fe50ad20-f759-5095-acdc-f9e5e8d0926d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153454 #edb/ref 1067 "Proving the Division Properties" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
